{% extends "@core/docs/_layout.twig" %}

{% block doc_content %}
    <h1>The Service Container</h1>
    <p>
        Rhapsody's <code>Rhapsody\Core\Container</code> is the framework's dependency injection (DI) container.
        That's a fancy way of saying: it's the thing that builds your objects for you and hands them their
        dependencies, so you don't have to wire everything up by hand.
    </p>
    <p>
        You tell the container two things: <strong>what</strong> you want (an interface, a class name, or a plain
        string alias like <code>'config'</code>) and <strong>how</strong> to build it (a concrete class name, or a
        function that returns the object). Then, whenever you need an object, you ask the container for it. If that
        object needs other objects to be constructed, the container figures those out too — recursively — by
        inspecting the class's constructor with PHP's Reflection API. In practice, you rarely have to write
        <code>new Something(...)</code> yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>What It Does</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Binds names to things.</strong> An interface or alias can be mapped to a concrete class or to a factory function (a closure).</li>
        <li><strong>Auto-wires constructor dependencies.</strong> If a class's constructor asks for <code>LoggerInterface</code> and <code>UserRepository</code>, the container will resolve both and pass them in — recursively, all the way down.</li>
        <li><strong>Detects circular dependencies.</strong> If <code>A</code> needs <code>B</code> and <code>B</code> needs <code>A</code>, the container stops immediately and tells you, instead of looping forever.</li>
        <li><strong>Supports singletons.</strong> <code>singleton()</code> bindings are built once and then reused on every later request.</li>
        <li><strong>Traces every resolution.</strong> Class name, how long it took, who asked for it — all logged for debugging.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Binding Dependencies</h2>

    <h3><code>bind()</code> — a fresh instance every time</h3>
    <p>
        <code>bind()</code> tells the container: <em>"When someone asks for this abstract name, build it like this."</em>
        A <strong>new</strong> instance is created on <strong>every</strong> <code>resolve()</code> call. Use this for
        things that shouldn't be shared, like a per-request <code>Request</code> object.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// Bind a class to a factory function
$container->bind(Request::class, fn() => new Request());

// Bind an interface to a concrete implementation
$container->bind(LoggerInterface::class, FileLogger::class);

// Bind a string alias to a factory function
$container->bind('db', function () {
    return new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=test', 'user', 'pwd');
});</code></pre>

    <h3><code>singleton()</code> — build once, reuse forever</h3>
    <p>
        <code>singleton()</code> works exactly like <code>bind()</code>, except the <strong>first</strong>
        <code>resolve()</code> call builds the instance and <strong>caches</strong> it. Every call after that returns
        the same object instead of running the factory again.
    </p>
    <p>
        Use this for anything expensive or stateful that should be shared for the life of the container — a database
        connection, the Twig environment, an event dispatcher.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$container->singleton(EntityManager::class, function ($container) {
    return new EntityManager($connection, $config);
});</code></pre>

    <h3><code>instance()</code> — register an object you already built</h3>
    <p>
        If you've already constructed the object yourself and just want the container to return that exact instance,
        use <code>instance()</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$logger = new Logger($config);
$container->instance(LoggerInterface::class, $logger);
// Every resolve(LoggerInterface::class) call now returns this exact object</code></pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Resolving Dependencies</h2>
    <p>
        <code>resolve()</code> (also available as <code>get()</code> — they're identical) builds and returns an
        instance of the given abstract:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$logger = $container->resolve(Logger::class);
$logger = $container->get(Logger::class); // same thing</code></pre>

    <p>
        If you want to check whether something has been explicitly bound <em>before</em> trying to resolve it, use
        <code>has()</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">if ($container->has('db')) {
    $connection = $container->get('db');
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Auto-Wiring</h3>
    <p>
        If a class was never explicitly bound, <code>resolve()</code> falls back to <strong>reflection</strong>: it
        looks at the class's constructor and, for each parameter, tries to resolve that parameter's type
        automatically.
    </p>
    <p>The rules are simple:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Type-hinted with a class or interface</strong> → resolved recursively.</li>
        <li><strong>Has a default value</strong> (like <code>$debug = false</code>) → that default is used.</li>
        <li><strong>Anything else</strong> → an exception is thrown, telling you exactly which parameter couldn't be resolved and where.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code class="language-php">class UserService
{
    public function __construct(
        private LoggerInterface $logger,
        private UserRepository $repository
    ) {}
}

// LoggerInterface and UserRepository are both resolved automatically.
// LoggerInterface needs a binding (see above); UserRepository doesn't,
// as long as its own constructor has no unresolvable dependencies.
$userService = $container->resolve(UserService::class);</code></pre>

    <div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4">
        <strong>Heads up:</strong> interfaces must be explicitly bound. PHP can't instantiate an interface on its
        own, so the container has no way to guess which concrete class you meant.
        <code>bind(LoggerInterface::class, FileLogger::class)</code> is what makes the example above work.
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2>Singleton Lifecycle</h2>
    <p>
        Two methods exist for managing already-resolved singletons <strong>without</strong> triggering a new
        resolution:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>resolved(string $abstract): bool</code> — returns <code>true</code> if this singleton has already been built and cached on this container.</li>
        <li><code>forgetSingleton(string $abstract): void</code> — drops the cached instance, so the next <code>resolve()</code> call rebuilds it from scratch.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        These matter most under a <strong>persistent-worker runtime</strong> (see <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/roadrunner">RoadRunner Persistent Workers</a>),
        where the container is built once and reused for the life of the worker. <code>forgetSingleton()</code> lets
        you recover a singleton that entered an unusable state mid-request — for example, an
        <code>EntityManager</code> that Doctrine has permanently closed after an unrecoverable ORM exception —
        without restarting the whole worker.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Circular Dependency Detection</h2>
    <p>
        If class <code>A</code> depends on <code>B</code>, and <code>B</code> depends back on <code>A</code>,
        resolving either one throws immediately rather than recursing forever:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// Throws: Circular dependency detected: App\A → App\B → App\A
$container->resolve(App\A::class);</code></pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Resolution Tracing</h2>
    <p>
        Every resolution — successful or not — is logged with timing and caller information. This is useful for
        hunting down slow or unexpected instantiations:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$trace = Container::getTrace();
foreach ($trace as $entry) {
    echo "Resolved {$entry['class']} in {$entry['duration']}ms (called by {$entry['called_by']})\n";
}

Container::resetTrace();        // clear the log
Container::setProxyMode(true);  // flag subsequent entries as proxy resolutions</code></pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>The Global Accessor</h2>
    <p>
        For the vast majority of classes, the container should be <strong>threaded in through the constructor</strong>
        — that's what auto-wiring is for, and it's what keeps a class's real dependencies visible and easy to test.
    </p>
    <p>
        But some infrastructure-level classes are routinely instantiated directly with <code>new</code> by
        application code (never resolved through the container itself) and only occasionally need to reach a bound
        service. The <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/pagination">Pagination</a> class rendering a custom Twig template
        is a good example. Forcing every caller of <code>new Pagination(...)</code> to also pass in a
        <code>Twig\Environment</code> just for that one optional feature would be worse than the alternative: a
        global accessor.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// End of bootstrap.php, after every bind()/singleton() call has run:
Container::setInstance($container);

// Anywhere else in the framework:
$twig = Container::getInstance()->get(Twig\Environment::class);

// Check without triggering an exception if nothing has been set yet:
if (Container::hasInstance()) { ... }

// Test isolation — clear the registered instance between tests:
Container::forgetInstance();</code></pre>

    <div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4">
        <strong>Important:</strong> call <code>setInstance()</code> <strong>at the end</strong> of
        <code>bootstrap.php</code>, after every <code>bind()</code> and <code>singleton()</code> call has run.
        Registering too early is the same footgun that previously caused the custom 404 page to render without Twig
        bound — a bare, unconfigured container captured before its bindings existed.
    </div>

    <h3>Pros and Cons</h3>
    <div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4">
        <strong>Pros:</strong> no need to thread the container through classes that only need it occasionally;
        avoids forcing every constructor along a call chain to accept and forward a dependency it doesn't itself use;
        works identically under a persistent-worker runtime, since <code>setInstance()</code> only ever needs to run
        once at boot.
    </div>
    <div class="bg-red-200 border-l-4 border-red-700 text-red-700 p-4">
        <strong>Cons:</strong> pulling a dependency out of a global hides it from a class's public API — a caller
        can no longer tell what a class needs just by reading its constructor. It also makes unit testing harder,
        since a test now has to call <code>Container::setInstance()</code> with a suitable double before exercising
        the class, rather than simply passing one in. <strong>Reserve <code>getInstance()</code> for classes the
        container doesn't itself resolve</strong> — anything the container builds for you (controllers, services
        bound via <code>bind()</code>/<code>singleton()</code>) should keep using constructor injection.
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong><code>bind()</code> always creates a new instance.</strong> If you want something shared, use <code>singleton()</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>No contextual binding.</strong> You can't say "give this class implementation X of the interface, but give that class implementation Y." One binding per abstract, period.</li>
        <li><strong>The resolution trace is static and shared.</strong> <code>getTrace()</code> returns the same log across every container instance in the process.</li>
        <li><strong>No implicit interface autowiring.</strong> An interface must be explicitly <code>bind()</code>'d to a concrete class before it can be resolved.</li>
    </ul>
{% endblock %}